1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,339
This video shows how to convert an N F A into a D F A. The method is the subset construction.

2
00:00:07,789 --> 00:00:21,527
We first find the idea: simulate the N F A and keep a set of states. Then we run the construction by hand on the machine for a or a b star. At the end we solve two past midterm questions.

3
00:00:21,977 --> 00:00:32,473
A lexer is compiled from regular expressions. The pipeline goes from a regex, to an N F A, to a D F A, to a minimal D F A.

4
00:00:32,923 --> 00:00:41,563
Step 1 used four templates, one per regex construct. The N F A is easy to build, but it is not easy to run.

5
00:00:42,013 --> 00:00:49,821
This video is step 2. It converts the machine that is easy to specify into a machine that is easy to execute.

6
00:00:50,271 --> 00:00:56,181
Step 3, minimization, makes the D F A as small as possible. That is the next video.

7
00:00:56,631 --> 00:01:03,969
This is the N F A for a or a b star, from the last video. It has ten states, numbered 1 to 10.

8
00:01:04,419 --> 00:01:06,489
State 9 is the start state.

9
00:01:06,939 --> 00:01:09,115
State 10 is the accepting state.

10
00:01:09,565 --> 00:01:14,130
States 1 to 6 are the three atom machines: a, a, and b.

11
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:18,719
States 7 and 8 come from the repetition template for b star.

12
00:01:19,169 --> 00:01:22,902
States 9 and 10 come from the alternation template.

13
00:01:23,352 --> 00:01:32,397
The machine is correct by construction. But from state 9 there are two epsilon edges. Nothing tells a program which one to take.

14
00:01:32,847 --> 00:01:37,327
Nondeterminism does not give finite automata extra power.

15
00:01:37,777 --> 00:01:44,753
For pushdown automata, nondeterminism does give extra power. Finite automata are different.

16
00:01:45,203 --> 00:01:55,123
We know this because we can translate every N F A into a D F A. This translation is not syntax directed. It is a bit more difficult.

17
00:01:55,573 --> 00:02:10,400
The D F A accepts the same language as the N F A. So a D F A can do everything that an N F A can do. Translation is the usual way to show that one formalism is no more powerful than another.

18
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:17,805
First, we run a D F A. Here is a D F A for the same language. We build it later in this video.

19
00:02:18,255 --> 00:02:28,601
The program needs one variable, state. For each input letter, it looks up the next state in the table delta. At the end, it checks if the state is accepting.

20
00:02:29,051 --> 00:02:32,422
Run the input a b b. The state starts at A.

21
00:02:32,872 --> 00:02:35,304
Read a. The table sends A to B.

22
00:02:35,754 --> 00:02:38,463
Read b. The table sends B to C.

23
00:02:38,913 --> 00:02:42,177
Read the last b. The table sends C to C.

24
00:02:42,627 --> 00:02:53,273
The input is finished, and C is accepting, so the answer is yes. One variable and one lookup per letter. That is why a D F A is our target language.

25
00:02:53,723 --> 00:03:05,051
Now the exercise from the slides. You get the N F A and the input a b b. Write a program that answers yes or no. The program must keep something in memory.

26
00:03:05,501 --> 00:03:11,773
The N F A can be in several states at once. So the program keeps all the states it can be in.

27
00:03:12,223 --> 00:03:14,932
At the start, the machine is in state 9.

28
00:03:15,382 --> 00:03:22,721
It can also follow the epsilon edges for free, to 1 and to 3. So the set is 1, 3, 9.

29
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:27,523
Read a. State 1 goes to 2, and state 3 goes to 4.

30
00:03:27,973 --> 00:03:31,365
Then follow the epsilon edges. 2 reaches 10.

31
00:03:31,815 --> 00:03:38,556
4 reaches 7, then 5 and 8, then 10. The set is 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10.

32
00:03:39,006 --> 00:03:49,694
State 10 was reached on two paths: from 2 and from 8. Two paths that reach the same state are the same situation. So we keep a set, not a list.

33
00:03:50,144 --> 00:03:53,877
Read b. Only state 5 has a b edge, to 6.

34
00:03:54,327 --> 00:04:00,621
From 6, the epsilon edges reach 5, 8 and 10. The set is 5, 6, 8, 10.

35
00:04:01,071 --> 00:04:07,812
Read the last b. Again only 5 moves to 6, and the closure gives 5, 6, 8, 10.

36
00:04:08,262 --> 00:04:12,699
A set of ten states fits in ten bits, so the memory is bounded.

37
00:04:13,149 --> 00:04:19,485
The input is finished. The set contains state 10, which is accepting. So the answer is yes.

38
00:04:19,935 --> 00:04:29,813
This is the whole construction. A set of N F A states is a D F A state. For example, the set 1, 3, 9 is the D F A state A.

39
00:04:30,263 --> 00:04:36,428
The simulator that you just wrote computes the next set while it reads the input: a, then b.

40
00:04:36,878 --> 00:04:49,977
The subset construction computes the same sets in advance, and writes them down as the D F A table. The simulator is the D F A. We only write its table before the input arrives.

41
00:04:50,427 --> 00:05:02,245
Here are the rules. Step 1. The start state A is the N F A start state, plus every state that it reaches by epsilon edges. We write this as epsilon star.

42
00:05:02,695 --> 00:05:17,501
Step 2. Take a state you built and a letter x. From its N F A states, take every x edge. Then close the result under epsilon edges. We write this as x epsilon star. That set is where x takes you.

43
00:05:17,951 --> 00:05:26,569
Step 3. Repeat until no new sets appear. A D F A state is accepting if it contains an accepting N F A state.

44
00:05:27,019 --> 00:05:33,057
The empty set is a state like any other. It is the error state. Every letter keeps you in it.

45
00:05:33,507 --> 00:05:46,861
Be careful with the order. It is x epsilon star, not epsilon star x. An epsilon edge does not mean skip an input letter. It means the machine may jump for free, and then read.

46
00:05:47,311 --> 00:05:53,733
Take the set B, which we build soon: 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10. Take the letter b.

47
00:05:54,183 --> 00:05:58,705
First take the b edges. Only state 5 has one, to state 6.

48
00:05:59,155 --> 00:06:07,667
Then close under epsilon edges. From 6 we reach 5, 8 and 10. The answer is C: 5, 6, 8, 10.

49
00:06:08,117 --> 00:06:15,349
A common error is to keep the old states, or to close them again. That gives a set with 2, 4 and 7.

50
00:06:15,799 --> 00:06:21,581
But 2, 4 and 7 have no b edge. So after a b, the machine cannot be there.

51
00:06:22,031 --> 00:06:35,257
A state that you land in adds its epsilon closure. A state that you were in already had its closure when you arrived. The slides say this is the step where most hand constructions go wrong.

52
00:06:35,707 --> 00:06:42,086
Now convert the a or a b star machine. First find the start state A of the D F A.

53
00:06:42,536 --> 00:06:45,373
Begin with the N F A start state, 9.

54
00:06:45,823 --> 00:06:49,130
From 9, the epsilon edges reach 1 and 3.

55
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:54,337
Neither 1 nor 3 has an epsilon edge out, so the closure stops.

56
00:06:54,787 --> 00:07:10,382
So A is the set 1, 3, 9. It does not contain 10, so A does not accept. We write it as the first row of the table. The order in which you explore is your choice. It changes only the state names, not the machine.

57
00:07:10,832 --> 00:07:14,032
Take A, the set 1, 3, 9, and the letter a.

58
00:07:14,482 --> 00:07:23,741
State 1 has an a edge to 2. State 3 has an a edge to 4. State 9 has no a edge. So the a edges give 2 and 4.

59
00:07:24,191 --> 00:07:27,860
Now close under epsilon edges. 2 reaches 10.

60
00:07:28,310 --> 00:07:35,051
4 reaches 7, then 5 and 8, then 10. The set is 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10.

61
00:07:35,501 --> 00:07:41,901
This is a new set. Call it B. It contains 10, so B accepts. Write: A on a goes to B.

62
00:07:42,351 --> 00:07:50,479
Now A on b. None of 1, 3, 9 has a b edge. The result is the empty set. Call it D, the error state.

63
00:07:50,929 --> 00:07:54,812
Next, B: the set 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10.

64
00:07:55,262 --> 00:07:59,998
B on a. None of these states has an a edge. B on a goes to D.

65
00:08:00,448 --> 00:08:04,459
B on b. Only state 5 has a b edge, to 6.

66
00:08:04,909 --> 00:08:07,831
Close: from 6 we reach 5, 8 and 10.

67
00:08:08,281 --> 00:08:14,319
The set 5, 6, 8, 10 is new. Call it C. It contains 10, so C accepts.

68
00:08:14,769 --> 00:08:18,267
C on a: no a edges, so C on a goes to D.

69
00:08:18,717 --> 00:08:27,272
C on b: again only 5 goes to 6, and the closure is the same set. So C on b goes to C, a self loop.

70
00:08:27,722 --> 00:08:37,023
Last, D, the empty set. It has no edges at all. Every letter keeps D in D. No new sets appear, so the construction stops.

71
00:08:37,473 --> 00:08:43,404
Now draw the D F A from the table. The start state is A, the set 1, 3, 9.

72
00:08:43,854 --> 00:08:46,094
A on a goes to B, which accepts.

73
00:08:46,544 --> 00:08:49,083
A on b goes to D, the empty set.

74
00:08:49,533 --> 00:08:51,943
B on b goes to C, which accepts.

75
00:08:52,393 --> 00:08:54,143
C on b stays in C.

76
00:08:54,593 --> 00:08:56,790
B on a and C on a go to D.

77
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:07,032
D loops to itself on a and b. Now every state has exactly one arrow per letter. Ten N F A states gave four D F A states.

78
00:09:07,482 --> 00:09:11,898
Do two quick checks before you trust a construction that you did by hand.

79
00:09:12,348 --> 00:09:23,249
Check 1. Is it a D F A at all? Every state must have an arrow on every letter. A missing arrow means you missed a step. It does not mean the machine rejects.

80
00:09:23,699 --> 00:09:28,393
Check 2. Test it. Pick an accepting state, and a string that reaches it.

81
00:09:28,843 --> 00:09:39,019
Take a b b. The D F A goes A, B, C, C, and C accepts. The regex matches a b b too: a b b is a, then b star.

82
00:09:39,469 --> 00:09:48,919
Now a rejected string, b a. The D F A goes A, D, D, and D does not accept. The regex does not match b a either.

83
00:09:49,369 --> 00:10:00,249
This is testing. The regex is the specification. The D F A is the implementation. Pick inputs that cover different paths, run both, and compare.

84
00:10:00,699 --> 00:10:07,846
Now a past midterm question. Convert this N F A into a D F A, and show the final states.

85
00:10:08,296 --> 00:10:10,003
State 1 is the start.

86
00:10:10,453 --> 00:10:12,671
State 8 is the final state.

87
00:10:13,121 --> 00:10:16,023
The dashed purple edges are epsilon edges.

88
00:10:16,473 --> 00:10:18,115
Pause the video and try it.

89
00:10:18,565 --> 00:10:22,917
Start state. From 1, the epsilon edges reach 2 and 5.

90
00:10:23,367 --> 00:10:26,375
From 2, an epsilon edge reaches 3.

91
00:10:26,825 --> 00:10:33,652
So the start state X is 1, 2, 3, 5. It does not contain 8, so X is not final.

92
00:10:34,102 --> 00:10:39,094
X on a. State 2 goes to 3, and state 5 goes to 6.

93
00:10:39,544 --> 00:10:42,637
Close: 6 reaches 8 by an epsilon edge.

94
00:10:43,087 --> 00:10:48,741
The set 3, 6, 8 is new. Call it Y. It contains 8, so Y is final.

95
00:10:49,191 --> 00:10:53,799
X on b. State 3 has two b edges, to 2 and to 4.

96
00:10:54,249 --> 00:10:57,427
Close: 2 reaches 3, and 4 reaches 5.

97
00:10:57,877 --> 00:11:01,355
The set 2, 3, 4, 5 is new. Call it Z.

98
00:11:01,805 --> 00:11:10,743
Next, Y: the set 3, 6, 8. Y on b. The b edges are 3 to 2, 3 to 4, 6 to 7, and 8 to 5.

99
00:11:11,193 --> 00:11:14,372
Close: 2 reaches 3, and 4 reaches 5.

100
00:11:14,822 --> 00:11:22,139
The set 2, 3, 4, 5, 7 is new. Call it W. It has no 8, so W is not final.

101
00:11:22,589 --> 00:11:30,077
Y on a. None of 3, 6, 8 has an a edge. So Y on a goes to the empty set, the error state.

102
00:11:30,527 --> 00:11:37,077
Next, Z: the set 2, 3, 4, 5. On a, 2 goes to 3, and 5 goes to 6.

103
00:11:37,527 --> 00:11:41,857
Close: 6 reaches 8. That is 3, 6, 8, which is Y.

104
00:11:42,307 --> 00:11:45,443
Z on b: 3 goes to 2 and to 4.

105
00:11:45,893 --> 00:11:52,656
Close: 2 reaches 3, and 4 reaches 5. That is 2, 3, 4, 5, which is Z again.

106
00:11:53,106 --> 00:12:01,831
Next, W: the set 2, 3, 4, 5, 7. On a, 2 goes to 3, 5 goes to 6, and 7 goes to 8.

107
00:12:02,281 --> 00:12:08,297
Close: 6 reaches 8, which is in the set already. That is 3, 6, 8, which is Y.

108
00:12:08,747 --> 00:12:12,481
W on b: only 3 moves, to 2 and to 4.

109
00:12:12,931 --> 00:12:19,245
Close: 2 reaches 3, and 4 reaches 5. That is 2, 3, 4, 5, which is Z.

110
00:12:19,695 --> 00:12:28,869
The empty set goes to itself on both letters. No new sets appear. The D F A has X, Y, Z, W, and the error state.

111
00:12:29,319 --> 00:12:36,444
Here is the D F A. X is the start state. Each state has one arrow on a and one arrow on b.

112
00:12:36,894 --> 00:12:42,291
Y is the only final state, because only Y contains N F A state 8.

113
00:12:42,741 --> 00:12:50,293
The posted solution does not draw the empty set. The slides ask for a complete D F A, so we draw it.

114
00:12:50,743 --> 00:12:56,546
A checker in python ran both machines on every string up to length 9. They agree.

115
00:12:56,996 --> 00:13:05,764
One more past midterm question. The regex is U or F or T, star. Here is the N F A from the posted solution.

116
00:13:06,214 --> 00:13:12,379
The two long dashed edges are epsilon edges: 8 back to 1, and 1 forward to 8.

117
00:13:12,829 --> 00:13:14,472
Pause the video and try it.

118
00:13:14,922 --> 00:13:22,837
Start state A. From 1, the epsilon edges reach 2, 3, 4 and 8. So A is 1, 2, 3, 4, 8.

119
00:13:23,287 --> 00:13:25,505
A contains 8, so A accepts.

120
00:13:25,955 --> 00:13:29,283
A on U. Only 2 has a U edge, to 5.

121
00:13:29,733 --> 00:13:38,459
Close: 5 reaches 8, 8 reaches 1, and 1 reaches 2, 3 and 4. So B is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8.

122
00:13:38,909 --> 00:13:44,562
On F, the same thing happens through 6. C is 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8.

123
00:13:45,012 --> 00:13:49,215
On T, through 7. D is 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8.

124
00:13:49,665 --> 00:13:53,398
Every set contains 8, so all four states accept.

125
00:13:53,848 --> 00:13:59,949
From every state, U goes to B, F goes to C, and T goes to D. No new sets appear.

126
00:14:00,399 --> 00:14:06,159
Here is the D F A: four states, A to D, with one arrow per letter from each state.

127
00:14:06,609 --> 00:14:10,172
Every state accepts, because every set contains 8.

128
00:14:10,622 --> 00:14:22,931
The language is every string over U, F and T. So the four states cannot be told apart. Minimization merges them into one state, A B C D. That is the next video.

129
00:14:23,381 --> 00:14:32,491
Each D F A state is a subset of the N F A states. Here the N F A has 10 states, so a subset is a row of 10 cells.

130
00:14:32,941 --> 00:14:37,741
Each cell is in the subset or not. That is 2 choices for each state.

131
00:14:38,191 --> 00:14:49,199
So there are 2 times 2 times 2, ten times, which is 2 to the 10, or 1024 subsets. For n states, there are 2 to the n subsets.

132
00:14:49,649 --> 00:14:58,715
The course notes say that in the worst case the D F A is exponentially larger than the N F A. This rarely happens in practice.

133
00:14:59,165 --> 00:15:09,960
In our example, only 4 of the 1024 subsets are reachable: A, B, C and D. The construction builds only reachable sets.

134
00:15:10,410 --> 00:15:17,535
To summarize. An N F A can be in a set of states, and a set of N F A states is a D F A state.

135
00:15:17,985 --> 00:15:22,615
The start state is the epsilon closure of the N F A start state.

136
00:15:23,065 --> 00:15:31,918
For each state and letter x, take the x edges, then close under epsilon. x epsilon star, not epsilon star x.

137
00:15:32,368 --> 00:15:39,557
A D F A state accepts if it contains an accepting N F A state. The empty set is the error state.

138
00:15:40,007 --> 00:15:51,997
Check your work. Every state needs one arrow per letter, and test strings must agree with the N F A and the regex. Next video: make the D F A as small as possible.
